17:     executed as it is read.  (If a file contains a command to halt the
18:     processor, bc will never read from the standard input.)
20:     This version of bc contains several extensions beyond traditional bc
21:     implementations and the POSIX draft standard.  Command line options can
22:     cause these extensions to print a warning or to be rejected.  This
23:     document describes the language accepted by this processor.  Extensions
24:     will be identified as such.
49:     The most basic element in bc is the number.  Numbers are arbitrary
50:     precision numbers.  This precision is both in the integer part and the
51:     fractional part.  All numbers are represented internally in decimal and
52:     all computation is done in decimal.  (This version truncates results from
53:     divide and multiply operations.)  There are two attributes of numbers,
54:     the length and the scale.  The length is the total number of decimal
55:     digits used by bc to represent a number and the scale is the total number
56:     of decimal digits after the decimal point.  For example:
61:     Numbers are stored in two types of variables, simple variables and
62:     arrays.  Both simple variables and array variables are named.  Names
63:     begin with a letter followed by any number of letters, digits and
64:     underscores.  All letters must be lower case.  (Full alpha-numeric names
65:     are an extension. In POSIX bc all names are a single lower case letter.)
66:     The type of variable is clear by the context because all array variable
67:     names will be followed by brackets ([]).
69:     There are four special variables, scale, ibase, obase, and last.  scale
70:     defines how some operations use digits after the decimal point.  The
71:     default value of scale is 0. ibase and obase define the conversion base
72:     for input and output numbers.  The default for both input and output is
73:     base 10.  last (an extension) is a variable that has the value of the
74:     last printed number.  These will be discussed in further detail where
75:     appropriate.  All of these variables may have values assigned to them as
76:     well as used in expressions.
79:     Comments in bc start with the characters /* and end with the characters
81:     input.  (This causes comments to delimit other input items.  For example,
82:     a comment can not be found in the middle of a variable name.)  Comments
83:     include any newlines (end of line) between the start and the end of the
84:     comment.
86:     To support the use of scripts for bc, a single line comment has been
87:     added as an extension.  A single line comment starts at a # character and
88:     continues to the next end of the line.  The end of line character is not
89:     part of the comment and is processed normally.
91:   EXPRESSIONS
92:     The numbers are manipulated by expressions and statements.  Since the
93:     language was designed to be interactive, statements and expressions are
94:     executed as soon as possible.  There is no "main" program.  Instead, code
95:     is executed as it is encountered.  (Functions, discussed in detail later,
96:     are defined when encountered.)
98:     A simple expression is just a constant. bc converts constants into
99:     internal decimal numbers using the current input base, specified by the
100:     variable ibase. (There is an exception in functions.)  The legal values
101:     of ibase are 2 through 36. (Bases greater than 16 are an extension.)
102:     Assigning a value outside this range to ibase will result in a value of 2
103:     or 36.  Input numbers may contain the characters 0-9 and A-Z. (Note: They
104:     must be capitals.  Lower case letters are variable names.)  Single digit
105:     numbers always have the value of the digit regardless of the value of
106:     ibase. (i.e. A = 10.)  For multi-digit numbers, bc changes all input
107:     digits greater or equal to ibase to the value of ibase-1.  This makes the
108:     number ZZZ always be the largest 3 digit number of the input base.
110:     Full expressions are similar to many other high level languages.  Since
111:     there is only one kind of number, there are no rules for mixing types.
112:     Instead, there are rules on the scale of expressions.  Every expression
113:     has a scale.  This is derived from the scale of original numbers, the
114:     operation performed and in many cases, the value of the variable scale.
115:     Legal values of the variable scale are 0 to the maximum number
116:     representable by a C integer.
