359-
360-     for ( [expression1] ; [expression2] ; [expression3] ) statement
361-            The for statement controls repeated execution of the statement.
362-            Expression1 is evaluated before the loop.  Expression2 is
363-            evaluated before each execution of the statement.  If it is non-
364-            zero, the statement is evaluated.  If it is zero, the loop is
365-            terminated.  After each execution of the statement, expression3 is
366-            evaluated before the reevaluation of expression2.  If expression1
367-            or expression3 are missing, nothing is evaluated at the point they
368-            would be evaluated.  If expression2 is missing, it is the same as
369-            substituting the value 1 for expression2.  (The optional
370-            expressions are an extension. POSIX bc requires all three
371-            expressions.)  The following is equivalent code for the for
372-            statement:
373-            expression1;
374-            while (expression2) {
375-               statement;
376-               expression3;
377-            }
378-
379-     break  This statement causes a forced exit of the most recent enclosing
380-            while statement or for statement.
381-
382-     continue
383-            The continue statement (an extension)  causes the most recent
384-            enclosing for statement to start the next iteration.
385-
386:     halt   The halt statement (an extension) is an executed statement that
387-            causes the bc processor to quit only when it is executed.  For
388-            example, "if (0 == 1) halt" will not cause bc to terminate because
389-            the halt is not executed.
390-
391-     return Return the value 0 from a function.  (See the section on
