399-   PSEUDO STATEMENTS
400-     These statements are not statements in the traditional sense.  They are
401-     not executed statements.  Their function is performed at "compile" time.
402-
403-     limits Print the local limits enforced by the local version of bc.  This
404-            is an extension.
405-
406:     quit   When the quit statement is read, the bc processor is terminated,
407:            regardless of where the quit statement is found.  For example, "if
408-            (0 == 1) quit" will cause bc to terminate.
409-
410-     warranty
411-            Print a longer warranty notice.  This is an extension.
412-
413-   FUNCTIONS
414-     Functions provide a method of defining a computation that can be executed
415-     later.  Functions in bc always compute a value and return it to the
416-     caller.  Function definitions are "dynamic" in the sense that a function
417-     is undefined until a definition is encountered in the input.  That
418-     definition is then used until another definition function for the same
419-     name is encountered.  The new definition then replaces the older
420-     definition.  A function is defined as follows:
421-            define name ( parameters ) { newline
422-                auto_list   statement_list }
423-     A function call is just an expression of the form "name(parameters)".
424-
425-     Parameters are numbers or arrays (an extension).  In the function
426-     definition, zero or more parameters are defined by listing their names
427-     separated by commas.  All parameters are call by value parameters.
428-     Arrays are specified in the parameter definition by the notation
429-     "name[]".   In the function call, actual parameters are full expressions
430-     for number parameters.  The same notation is used for passing arrays as
431-     for defining array parameters.  The named array is passed by value to the
432-     function.  Since function definitions are dynamic, parameter numbers and
433-     types are checked when a function is called.  Any mismatch in number or
434-     types of parameters will cause a runtime error.  A runtime error will
435-     also occur for the call to an undefined function.
436-
437-     The auto_list is an optional list of variables that are for "local" use.
438-     The syntax of the auto list (if present) is "auto name, ... ;".  (The
439-     semicolon is optional.)  Each name is the name of an auto variable.
440-     Arrays may be specified by using the same notation as used in parameters.
441-     These variables have their values pushed onto a stack at the start of the
442-     function.  The variables are then initialized to zero and used throughout
443-     the execution of the function.  At function exit, these variables are
444-     popped so that the original value (at the time of the function call) of
445-     these variables are restored.  The parameters are really auto variables
446-     that are initialized to a value provided in the function call.  Auto
447-     variables are different than traditional local variables because if
448-     function A calls function B, B may access function A's auto variables by
449-     just using the same name, unless function B has called them auto
450-     variables.  Due to the fact that auto variables and parameters are pushed
451-     onto a stack, bc supports recursive functions.
452-
453-     The function body is a list of bc statements.  Again, statements are
454-     separated by semicolons or newlines.  Return statements cause the
455-     termination of a function and the return of a value.  There are two
456-     versions of the return statement.  The first form, "return", returns the
457-     value 0 to the calling expression.  The second form, "return ( expression
458-     )", computes the value of the expression and returns that value to the
459-     calling expression.  There is an implied "return (0)" at the end of every
460-     function.  This allows a function to terminate and return 0 without an
461-     explicit return statement.
462-
